#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092025 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "3ea5361d-2508-5d54-acbf-46a57be601f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5483" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=2$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda =2\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix} - 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{5x_{1} - 4x_{2} = 0; 5x_{1} - 4x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable.\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n5x_1-4x_2=0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1=\\dfrac{4}{5}x_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x_2=5$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092026 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc3c9f32-7ac7-5c2c-b337-70fcfbc802b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "f23dcdbe-03e6-582f-8383-b07a1b2036c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5484" :question/problem "If $\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=1$, then what is the value of $\\,\\dfrac{b}{a}\\,$?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda =1\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 5x_{2} = 0; - x_{1} - 5x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable.\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1+5x_2=0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1=-5x_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=-1$, we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{b}{a}=\\dfrac{-1}{5}=-\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092027 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "b59a8ad2-7233-54c2-9055-5e188e0b8503" :question/math-academy-id "e-5485" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda=1$ and $\\lambda=-4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A$." :question/worked-solution "We can get two linearly independent eigenvectors by simply finding eigenvectors corresponding to the different eigenvalues.\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately.\n\n- For $\\lambda =1$, we have\n$A - λI|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} - 1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n${-x_{2} = 0; - 5x_{2} = 0$\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n$M|= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-5)R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable.\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0$. Therefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nSetting, for instance, $x_1=1$, we get the eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-4$, we have\n$A - λI|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} - (-4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n${5x_{1} - x_{2} = 0; 0 = 0$\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n$M|= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn the matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable.\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{1}{5}x_2$. Therefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=5$, we get the eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}]}
